tests/fwrite -- probes for fwrite in include/stdio.h

real.c, real.sh   REAL: `ulimit -f 1`, SIGXFSZ ignored; the kernel really
                  refuses bytes.  Native, -run, and Python --drive built.

sim.sh            SIMULATED: the authoritative simulated test.  It extracts
                  the current `static long fwrite(` definition from
                  include/stdio.h (awk, through the matching closing brace),
                  renames it fwrite_under_test, textually replaces its one
                  `__write(` call with a recording shim, and runs 11 cases on
                  all three paths.  The shim records each call's (offset
                  from buf, n, return), and the probe asserts them.
                  This tests the CURRENT library function body as compiled
                  by each front end.  It does NOT test macro-expansion
                  consistency (see the known gap below).
                  usage: tests/fwrite/sim.sh [ref-compiler] [stdio.h]

sim.c             SIMULATED, SUPERSEDED by sim.sh.  It relies on
                  `#define __write shim` before `#include <stdio.h>`, which
                  only the Python front end honours.  On native and -run the
                  shim is never called, and 6 of its 11 cases (complete,
                  short_then_ok, short_then_err, zero_stops, round_down,
                  over_answer) FAIL there with calls=0.  The 5 that print
                  "ok" natively (sz0, n0, overflow, negative_sz, negative_n)
                  expect zero calls, so they pass vacuously: the shim is
                  not reached at all, not "reached zero times".  Treat
                  native sim.c results as UNCOVERED, never as green.  No
                  runner invokes sim.c.  It is kept only as the Python-side
                  macro probe.

KNOWN GAP (separate from fwrite; not fixed here): the native preprocessor
does not expand a user macro, defined before #include, inside an
embedded-header function body WHEN THE PROGRAM USES THAT FUNCTION.  The
Python front end does expand it.

  -E reproduction (native):
    d.c:  #define __write shim
          #include <stdio.h>
          int main(void) { return 0; }
    g.c:  #define __write shim
          #include <stdio.h>
          int main(void) { return (int)fwrite("abc", 1, 3, stdout); }

    ua -E d.c | grep 'p + done'
      ->  r =  shim (_unisa_fd(f), (char *)p + done, total - done);   expanded
    ua -E g.c | grep 'p + done'
      ->  r = __write(_unisa_fd(f), (char *)p + done, total - done);  NOT expanded

  Behavioural reproduction (m.c):
    static long shim(int fd, char *p, long n) { return 42; }
    #define __write shim
    #include <stdio.h>
    int main(void) { return (int)fwrite("abc", 1, 3, stdout) == 3 ? 0 : 1; }

    ua -run m.c                            -> prints "abc", exit 0
                                              (real __write; macro ignored)
    python3 -m unisa run m.c --drive built -> no output, exit 1
                                              (shim called; 42 > 3 = error)

  Measured 2026-09-25 with a ua built by tests/build_ref.sh.
